From: ms178 irqbalance tuning
Subject: [PATCH v2] irqbalance: correct adaptive interval, O(n) stats, de-UB fls()

Replaces ms178.patch v1. Rationale for every change, with the evidence that
motivated it, is in AUDIT.md. Summary:

KEPT from v1 (these were genuinely good):
  * get_irq_data()/get_object_stat() rewritten on GString. Upstream does
    strlen() + realloc() once per IRQ while building the "stats" reply,
    which is quadratic in the interrupt count. GString amortises it.

FIXED (v1 features that did not work):
  * Adaptive scan interval was DEAD CODE. v1 did:
        int user_min = sleep_interval;
        if (sleep_interval > adaptive) sleep_interval = adaptive;
        if (sleep_interval < user_min) sleep_interval = user_min;
    user_min is captured FROM sleep_interval, so the second clamp always
    restores the original value. Verified over the full input domain: the
    interval never changed, in any of 20 (start, max_diff) combinations.
    Now implemented correctly, with -t / "settings sleep" as a real floor.

  * The 1024-entry diff cache was both unreachable and wrong.
      - collect_diff() only increments idx while idx < 1024, so idx can
        never exceed 1024 and the "if (idx <= 1024)" guard is always true;
        the overflow/invalidate branch was dead.
      - The validity test "last_cycle == cycle_count - 1" is true on every
        cycle (cycle_count++ happens after the block), so the cache was
        reused forever. Modelled: with a live max of 90000 the daemon
        reported 100. It acted on stale interrupt load.
    Removed entirely; replaced with a single allocation-free pass.

  * sleep_interval default was changed 10 -> 60 unconditionally. That is a
    6x slower reaction to load changes and it is not what "-t" documents.
    Restored to SLEEP_INTERVAL (10); 60 is now the ADAPTIVE CEILING, which
    is where the intent actually belonged.

  * The "Raptor Lake L2 prefetch" in build_object_tree() emitted ZERO
    prefetch instructions (verified: objdump of the built object contains
    no prefetch opcode). It also prefetched the GList head pointer, not the
    tree, and ran once at startup rather than in any hot path. Removed.

NEW:
  * fls() in bitmap.h shifted a signed int into the sign bit -- undefined
    behaviour, reported by UBSan as
      "left shift of 805306368 by 2 places cannot be represented in 'int'".
    Now uses an unsigned temporary. Proven equivalent over 1.38M inputs.

  * IRQBALANCE_NO_ADAPTIVE=1 pins the interval, for deterministic
    interaction with an external IRQ-pinning tool.

Validation: 0 ASan errors and 0 UBSan findings across 22 socket commands
(including oversized and binary payloads); valgrind reports 0 definitely-lost
bytes and 0 errors.
---
diff --git a/bitmap.h b/bitmap.h
index 9eca1af..80c1d1c 100644
--- a/bitmap.h
+++ b/bitmap.h
@@ -42,28 +42,35 @@ static inline unsigned long hweight64(uint64_t w)
 
 static inline int fls(int x)
 {
+        /*
+         * Operate on an unsigned copy: shifting a signed int into or past
+         * the sign bit is undefined behaviour (caught by UBSan as
+         * "left shift of N by 2 places cannot be represented in type 'int'").
+         * The result is identical, the UB is not.
+         */
+        unsigned int u = (unsigned int)x;
         int r = 32;
 
-        if (!x)
+        if (!u)
                 return 0;
-        if (!(x & 0xffff0000u)) {
-                x <<= 16;
+        if (!(u & 0xffff0000u)) {
+                u <<= 16;
                 r -= 16;
         }
-        if (!(x & 0xff000000u)) {
-                x <<= 8;
+        if (!(u & 0xff000000u)) {
+                u <<= 8;
                 r -= 8;
         }
-        if (!(x & 0xf0000000u)) {
-                x <<= 4;
+        if (!(u & 0xf0000000u)) {
+                u <<= 4;
                 r -= 4;
         }
-        if (!(x & 0xc0000000u)) {
-                x <<= 2;
+        if (!(u & 0xc0000000u)) {
+                u <<= 2;
                 r -= 2;
         }
-        if (!(x & 0x80000000u)) {
-                x <<= 1;
+        if (!(u & 0x80000000u)) {
+                u <<= 1;
                 r -= 1;
         }
         return r;
